.TP
.B pmap 1234
Default view: map of process 1234.
.TP
.B pmap \-x 1234
Extended view with RSS and dirty-page counts. Useful when you want
to know which mappings are actually paying for physical memory, not
just reserving virtual address space.
.TP
.B pmap \-d 1234
Device view: shows the file offset and \fImajor\fR\fB:\fR\fIminor\fR
device for each mapping.
.TP
.B pmap \-p \-x 1234
Extended view with full paths in the Mapping column. Without
\fB\-p\fR, only basenames are shown.
.TP
.B pmap \-q $(pgrep \-x my\-daemon)
Print the maps of every process named \fImy\-daemon\fR, with no
per-process header or footer. The \fB\-q\fR flag plus the
\fBpgrep\fR(1) substitution make this suitable for piping into
\fBgrep\fR or other filters.
.TP
.B pmap \-x 1234 5678 | sort \-rnk2
Sort the mappings by size (the \fIKbytes\fR column) to find the
largest ones across two processes.
